ing the preseason as  opposed to the regular season have you  seen this data Jerry Jones talked about  that and I do we have the exact wording  on that mclovin just to be fair to Jerry  Jones yeah he said there's a study if  they eliminate two preseason games and  add two regular-season games  it will actually added health B's or  more injuries or imprecision seaton do  you have this sound so I can play it for  Eric here so Jerry Jones my solution is  real simple is cut back on preseason  games have one at each team's home play  a couple of them and then you need to  add two games to the regular season  which I've been a proponent of for  several years it's probably physically  better for players than it is to have  the longer preseason the longer  practicing our studies showed that we  actually have a ramped up injury  situation with players during preseason  have you seen this data yet yet yet lies  damned lies and statistics right you  know it listen what he's referring to I  believe and this is the this is the data  I've seen is what he's referring to is a  heightened concussion rate or heightened  injury rate in the preseason because you  have 90 guys on a roster in the  preseason some of them which are they're  the first time in their NFL life they  don't know what they're doing  obviously indecision leads to injury and  then in the regular season you have  practices in which you only hit once a  week during this season right now you're  hitting six times a week so of course  you could say oh well there's more  injury rate there's an INT there's a  greater injury occurrence and the  preseason then there isn't the regular  season we all know that's not really how  you would read the data if you were sort  of being honest about it let's face it  and and how and how that ends up shaking  out so I mean let's just look at it from  a common-sense standpoint does does  anybody think playing more get to more  games it's safer for players know though  to act like playing to more games it's  safer for players is it's just to me a  pretty silly comment we're talking to  Eric Winston he's the president of the  NFL Players Association where do we  stand with the anthem feels like it's  kind of quiet on both sides here what's  gonna happen opening weekend  you know yeah I don't know I don't I  don't go out and tell guys you know you  have to do this or you have to do that I  think that's you know they they make  their decisions I can tell you right now  that that the two sides have been  talking we've been talking about a  resolution around this issue and we've  been talking about you know making sure  that the guys especially the guys that  have protested have a forum so they can  they can come in and directly speak to  some of us ownership about what exactly  they're trying to do and I think a lot  of guys are looking to move from from  where they've been to action now they  want something or they want to make it  substantive they want to make it lasting  and I applaud that because I think at  the end of the day that's what our guys  have always been about is really making  some sense of changes in the  neighborhood's they've grew up growing  up in and the society that they see  around so we're still talking about it  we're still trying to figure out exactly  what it looks like but I can but I think  as you said things have been quiet  because we have been talking we've been  trying to give ourselves the distance to  make sure that when we come to some sort  of resolution it is lasting and that  it's meaningful isn't this more team by  team Eric than it is the overall  perspective of the NFL or the Players  Association that it's the owner dealing  with his team dealing with his local  market it's just that you want to  appease your fan base there that the  owner is you know Jerry Jones is only  worried about dak Prescott in Ezekiel  Elliott standing for the anthem he wants  his star player he wants everybody  standing here I don't think he's as  concerned about the rest of the league  as he is how does this come off to my  fan base Jacksonville has it come off to  that fan base so it feels like it's more  you know team by team of how these  owners are approaching this can only  affect their their team their locales  but I will tell you that a lot of  ownership feels that that football is  not view that way that it's a national  game that were we're a national pastime  now and we're America's top sports so we  also you can also say that hey fan bases  if you're in New York you watch the  Monday night game between the Chargers  and the Raiders or whomever is playing  and  as you have in other sports you have  where it's a very local sport like  you're talking about now obviously  ownership can only affect that but I  would tell you that ownership is it's  concerned with what's going around the  league and that ex-owner in one locale  is worried about what's going on in  other locales even though they might be  able to only affect their team I would  say they're worried about what's going  on throughout the league and I  understand that but at the same time  like I said these guys are trying to  effectuate some positive change in their  neighborhoods and in the society as I  see and so you know we're facilitating a  lot of these discussions and like I said  I'm still hopeful Erika thank you we  appreciate your time thanks for joining  us all right on it's Eric Winston he's  the NFL Players Association for more Dan  Patrick show to the audience Channel 239  on DirecTV or download the Dan Patrick  show app  [Music]
 

